      \field{annotation}{Corrosion of zirconium alloys is a key issue for the overall nuclear industry that has been studied under various temperatures for plant operation, severe accidents, storage, and final disposal of spent cladding. The present work aims to investigate the corrosion kinetics of zirconium alloys at intermediate temperature regions between the operation and storage/disposal conditions, to examine if the extrapolation of high temperature data to lower temperatures is valid. To achieve this, an autoclave experiment, which is a common way to study corrosion behavior, was applied for up to 485 days at 4 different temperatures: 180 deg. C, 210 deg. C, 240 deg. C and 270 deg. C. The weight gain changes due to the oxide growth of the PWR cladding tube (Zircaloy-4) showed that the corrosion kinetics in temperatures above 210 deg. C follow the cubic rate law. The activation energy evaluated by the Arrhenius relationship of the cubic rate constants was determined and found to be 132.8 ± 0.6 kJ/mol, which corresponds to that generally reported under the operation temperatures. However, the kinetics at 180 deg. C was found to be close to the parabolic rate law, which suggests the kinetics changes from cubic to parabolic around 200 deg. C. Thus, it is unlikely to predict accurately long-term corrosion behavior under storage and disposal conditions by simply extrapolating the high temperature corrosion data. (authors)}
      \field{month}{07}
      \field{title}{Autoclave Corrosion Experiment of Zircaloy-4 at Temperatures Between 180 deg. C and 270 deg. C - 19167}
